WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.400
Both: Hee-hee-hoo, hee-hee-hoo.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.375
The whole breathing
technique that she's doing,

00:00:05.375 --> 00:00:07.000
with the "hee-hee-hoo,"

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.920
I've never actually
heard that in my career.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.436
Hi, I am Dr. Ali Rodriguez.

00:00:12.436 --> 00:00:14.720
I am an OB-GYN.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.761
Today we're going to be looking

00:00:16.761 --> 00:00:19.561
at pregnancy scenes in movies and in TV

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:21.635
and judging them for realism.

00:00:21.635 --> 00:00:23.727
Nurse: That's it. The
doctor will be right in.

00:00:23.727 --> 00:00:25.177
Jane: Thank you.
Rafael: Thank you.

00:00:25.177 --> 00:00:27.830
You know, I think they did
do a good job in this one

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.030
in making the baby bump look real.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.492
I think, I mean, it's believable.

00:00:31.492 --> 00:00:33.300
I think she looks pregnant to me.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.108
Let's check again in two weeks.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.580
How big the belly gets.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.810
When we do fundal height,

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:48.867
that's the measurement from the pubic bone

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:51.020
all the way to the top of the uterus.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.826
The top of the uterus
is called the fundus,

00:00:52.826 --> 00:00:54.510
which is why it's called fundal height.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.008
That is something that's
typically not done standing up.

00:00:58.008 --> 00:00:59.540
It would be very uncomfortable

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.622
and very difficult to do,
because the more, you know,

00:01:02.622 --> 00:01:05.160
obviously the bigger you get in pregnancy,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.785
it's going to be harder to get down

00:01:06.785 --> 00:01:09.470
to that pubic bone accurately

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.080
from the squatting
position that she's doing.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.180
Instead of the belly
growing, like, outward,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.393
we'd expect it to grow almost up,

00:01:18.393 --> 00:01:20.590
because the uterus and
baby are growing up.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.170
But currently the baby is breech.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.850
Head is up here, tush is down here.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.750
And the baby needs to be head
down for a natural delivery.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.580
I think the doctor does do a good job

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.330
of explaining what that means

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.850
and almost not to worry at this point.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.183
Throughout pregnancies,

00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:37.508
babies will turn from breech to cephalic,

00:01:37.508 --> 00:01:39.080
and they'll kind of do flips

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.050
and twirl around in there, and that's OK.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.920
Typically by the point that, you know,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.230
a patient is 33 weeks,
that's when we really

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.891
want baby to kind of start
moving towards head down,

00:01:50.891 --> 00:01:53.860
because, like she mentions
at the end of the clip,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.718
the bigger baby gets and the
closer you are to full term,

00:01:57.718 --> 00:01:59.060
they start running out of room.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.230
So if they haven't flipped by then,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.860
it's unlikely for them
to flip on their own,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.930
just because they just don't
have the space anymore.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.150
It looks very real.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.940
She looks pregnant to me, and
I think they did a good job.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.192
So, yeah, I'd say 7 out of 10.

00:02:13.192 --> 00:02:16.742
Get out! Oh, no.

00:02:16.742 --> 00:02:18.220
[screams]

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.080
Certainly birth can be traumatic.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.903
You know, a patient comes
in completely dilated

00:02:22.903 --> 00:02:25.660
or, you know, having really
bad contractions at home.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.670
That's when it can get a little traumatic,

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.800
but usually it's really not traumatic.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.500
Obviously it makes it more entertaining

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.345
and funny to have it be this way.

00:02:35.345 --> 00:02:38.960
Push! One, two, three.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.940
I always tell all of
my patients, you know,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.128
pushing and going through
labor is a lot of work,

00:02:45.128 --> 00:02:47.900
and you are going to
be pushing pretty hard.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.600
In order for it to be, I guess, effective,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.650
it's not necessarily
how hard you're pushing,

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.508
but are you using the right muscles?

00:02:56.508 --> 00:02:58.268
So, as funny as it sounds,

00:02:58.268 --> 00:03:00.757
the same muscles we use
to go to the bathroom

00:03:00.757 --> 00:03:04.118
are the same muscles we
use to push out a baby.

00:03:04.118 --> 00:03:05.829
[screams]

00:03:05.829 --> 00:03:06.662
Good!

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.063
Usually when we see clips in movies,

00:03:11.063 --> 00:03:12.563
you always have that blue sheet

00:03:12.563 --> 00:03:15.738
kind of draped over the legs.

00:03:15.738 --> 00:03:19.390
And, honestly, that's rarely ever used.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.900
I don't think I've ever used
it in any of the deliveries.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.953
The patient always has a
hospital gown on, definitely.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.652
Oh, that's a pretty big baby.

00:03:30.652 --> 00:03:32.980
I'd say the average size or weight

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:37.200
of a newborn is anywhere
between 6 to 7 pounds,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.470
and they're usually about,
like, 19 to 20 inches long.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.750
This baby to me appears to
be much bigger than that.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.022
I don't think they're actually using

00:03:45.022 --> 00:03:46.950
a newborn for these scenes.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.300
They definitely show,
like, this clean baby,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.570
and here it looks like they've
done a little bit of makeup.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:56.730
But typically babies are born
with something called vernix,

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.870
but this vernix, it almost serves

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:03.560
as a protective covering over fetus' skin

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.990
when they're in utero.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:07.835
It almost looks like a
really white, thick lotion.

00:04:07.835 --> 00:04:09.510
And I think that's what
they tried to kind of

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.440
put the makeup on the baby.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.270
As you can see, there's
white little chunks.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:14.750
Think of it as like a baby lotion.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.350
So it's OK to kind of
rub it into their skin.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:18.500
But I am gonna give it to them.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.570
I think they did a good job

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.690
of trying to put the makeup on the baby.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.511
I'd give it a 5 out of 10.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.687
One of the first presenting
symptoms of early pregnancy

00:04:32.687 --> 00:04:34.450
can be nausea and vomiting.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:37.840
A lot of my patients,
they'll cook a whole meal,

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.500
and then they sit down to eat it

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.440
and they just want to vomit.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.170
You have that surge of
estrogen, progesterone,

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.820
human -- or I guess hCG,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.390
which is a pregnancy hormone, increases,

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:51.682
and all of that, you know,

00:04:51.682 --> 00:04:54.182
there's a heightened sense of smell

00:04:54.182 --> 00:04:55.653
in women who are pregnant.

00:04:56.569 --> 00:04:57.827
[ripping]
[Bella screaming]

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.390
Seeing this much blood is not concerning.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.550
Seeing a vampire do the C-section

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.910
with his teeth is very
impressive and concerning.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.810
But, yeah, typically childbirth

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.080
is a process that is very bloody.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.880
We obviously don't want
them to lose too much,

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.960
which is why we still monitor vitals.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.060
And when you're closer to
that 40 weeks' gestation,

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.060
the uterus and the baby are getting

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:32.960
about 450 milliliters
of blood in a minute.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.770
After delivery, you still
have all of that blood

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:39.260
that's pumping to the uterus.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.910
So if there's some sort of, you know,

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.397
issue or something going on,

00:05:44.397 --> 00:05:47.630
that's a lot of blood that
a patient's going to lose

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:48.850
in a matter of seconds.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:50.640
Simply cannot handle this one,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.360
this gets a 1 out of 10.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.371
This is just too much, over the top,

00:05:55.371 --> 00:05:57.583
and very unrealistic.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.270
I just took the pregnancy test.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.170
Just tell me when a minute's up.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.730
I just put some pizza rolls
in the microwave oven.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.630
The minute that bad boy
dings, we're good to go.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.715
I think it'd be a good distraction from it

00:06:09.715 --> 00:06:11.410
because, you know, it's sometimes a moment

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:13.920
that can be very nerve-wracking

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.500
and cause a lot of anxiety.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.470
So I think distracting yourself

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.113
with something is a good idea.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.009
By the way, next time, buy
a name-brand pregnancy test.

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:25.380
Not that cheap generic one.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.370
Some of them are definitely advertised to

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:30.380
find out at the earliest
point in pregnancy.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:32.430
And that just simply means those ones

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.855
may be a little bit more sensitive

00:06:34.855 --> 00:06:36.980
to the level of pregnancy hormone.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:38.710
Most of these pregnancy tests that you buy

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:42.118
at the store, supermarket, or pharmacy,

00:06:42.118 --> 00:06:44.800
they're pushing, like,
99-percentile accuracy.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.025
So they're usually pretty good.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.303
Wait a second. Results in three minutes.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:52.890
How long should you wait?

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:55.710
Each brand and everything
like that will vary.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.020
Some of them are a minute.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.980
Some of them, you know, three minutes.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.830
Just really depends, but make sure

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.920
you read the packaging correctly

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.230
so that something like
this doesn't happen.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.080
You know, I think they did a great job.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.670
9 out of 10, everything
was pretty accurate.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.200
I think it's fun and obviously hysterical

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.877
that he finds the box and is like,

00:07:13.877 --> 00:07:16.116
"Oh, my goodness, three minutes."

00:07:16.116 --> 00:07:17.323
I have to sneeze.

00:07:18.769 --> 00:07:20.680
[sneezes]
[baby cries]

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.240
Wow. That's one baby out.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.220
Actually, this is something
that is very possible.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:26.511
I've had deliveries,

00:07:26.511 --> 00:07:29.136
especially when it's someone
who's a multigravida,

00:07:29.136 --> 00:07:31.710
which means she's had
multiple pregnancies.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:33.235
The more pregnancies you have,

00:07:33.235 --> 00:07:36.400
typically the faster the labor course is.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.060
And so I've had women who, you know,

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.543
it's their fifth, sixth baby,

00:07:40.543 --> 00:07:42.518
and they come in and, you know,

00:07:42.518 --> 00:07:44.003
once the baby's there and crowning,

00:07:44.003 --> 00:07:46.640
they just cough, and baby comes out.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.410
If this were her first
pregnancy, probably not.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.230
Your body's never done it before,

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:52.590
so it would be very difficult.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.440
Push. Here we go.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:59.310
Sometimes I find myself,
like, pushing almost for them,

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:02.040
because you just, you know,
you want to try and help.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:03.880
What ends up happening a
lot of the times though

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:06.890
that I've seen, when the
partners are like this,

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.330
it bothers the patient sometimes.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.049
And so they'll kind of be like, "Go away,"

00:08:11.049 --> 00:08:13.360
or, you know, "Don't stand next to me!"

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:15.020
You pull out this bar,

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.380
and it connects to the
bottom, like around the table.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:22.040
And so the purpose of that is instead of

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.656
you kind of laying on your back

00:08:24.656 --> 00:08:26.856
with your legs up in stirrups,

00:08:26.856 --> 00:08:30.110
this one kind of simulates
a position of squatting.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.180
Doctor: We need to
prepare for a C-section.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:35.144
No, no, no, no. No, I have a birth plan.

00:08:35.144 --> 00:08:36.750
I want to push. I want to push.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.093
She had this birth plan,

00:08:38.093 --> 00:08:40.690
but it seems like an emergency's going on.

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.540
The baby's heart rate was dropping.

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.500
So at that point it would be
an indication for a C-section.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:49.160
There is kind of this
saying on labor and delivery

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:53.230
that if you have this
rigid and set birth plan,

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:55.030
that it's going to end up in a C-section.

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:57.210
We're not saying it as a mean thing,

00:08:57.210 --> 00:09:00.490
but I think it's important
that when you are pregnant

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:01.970
and going in to have a baby,

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.060
you have to go with a mindset of,

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.060
yes, this is the birth plan that you want,

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.315
but you have to be able
to be flexible to change,

00:09:09.315 --> 00:09:12.110
because a lot of it is out of our control.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:13.270
You know, in this scene,

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:14.600
where the baby's heart rate's dropping,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.470
obviously no one wants to see that.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.255
They did a pretty good job with

00:09:18.255 --> 00:09:22.710
showing the different
scenarios that can take place.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.233
9 out of 10. They did good.

00:09:25.403 --> 00:09:26.619
Wanda: I can't do this.

00:09:26.619 --> 00:09:28.000
Yes you can.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:28.833
I can't.

00:09:28.833 --> 00:09:31.135
When, you know, she's obviously looking up

00:09:31.135 --> 00:09:34.590
and saying, "I can't do
this," and kind of panicking,

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.800
that is extremely accurate.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.780
It's almost like they go
into this, like, panic mode.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.220
I always like to tell them that,

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:48.120
yes, it is extremely
painful, this is temporary,

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.794
and they're going to feel so
much better once they deliver.

00:09:51.794 --> 00:09:54.510
Both: Hee-hee-hoo, hee-hee-hoo.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.500
The whole breathing
technique that she's doing,

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.100
with the "hee-hee-hoo,"

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:00.990
I've never actually
heard that in my career.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.808
It's just good to take
good, big, deep breaths,

00:10:03.808 --> 00:10:05.980
you know, in the nose,
out through the mouth,

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.090
kind of stay a little bit more calm.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.280
Laying flat on their back on the floor,

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:12.040
I don't think this is realistic.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.900
I don't find this position
to be comfortable.

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.820
Most women, whether
they're doing home births

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.530
or in the hospital, a lot of the times

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:23.080
they're still either squatting,

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:26.060
they can kind of be on
their back, but not flat,

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.108
almost just kind of propped up,

00:10:28.108 --> 00:10:30.160
almost like you're doing, like, ab workout

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.380
and you're kind of doing
that crunch position.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:34.120
I would say that's more comfortable

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:35.810
than just being flat on your back.

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.380
It's going to be very hard to breathe

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:38.596
and be very uncomfortable,

00:10:38.596 --> 00:10:40.054
because that's a lot of weight

00:10:40.054 --> 00:10:42.663
that's just kind of sitting on your chest.

00:10:43.616 --> 00:10:45.810
[baby crying]

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:48.485
Babies actually do cry
as soon as they're born,

00:10:48.485 --> 00:10:50.218
and we want them to cry.

00:10:50.218 --> 00:10:51.773
When they're in utero,

00:10:51.773 --> 00:10:55.060
their lungs aren't working, essentially.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:57.560
So once they are born,

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:58.860
they're kind of squeezed on their way

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.410
out of the birth canal,

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.170
and they give that first cry.

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.540
It actually helps them
kind of expand everything

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:07.770
and make sure everything's functioning.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:09.310
Sometimes they can swallow a little bit

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:10.310
of the amniotic fluid,

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:13.200
so by them kind of crying
and coughing it out,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.090
it's actually really helpful.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:17.820
That baby is spotless.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.070
That baby is very, very clean immediately.

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:24.800
Again, not something
that is realistic at all.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.463
So I'd have to say
either 1 or 2 out of 10.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.860
Cravings in pregnancy, very, very common.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:39.130
Especially in the first trimester,

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:40.300
it can start that early.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:42.560
And then the cravings can kind of change

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:44.255
as the pregnancy progresses.

00:11:44.255 --> 00:11:48.508
I have heard some very
interesting combinations of food.

00:11:48.508 --> 00:11:50.048
A lot of pickle juice,

00:11:50.048 --> 00:11:53.027
or pickles with, like,
chocolate, I've heard,

00:11:53.027 --> 00:11:56.900
which does not sound good to me at all.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.400
Go ahead and treat yourself.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:11:59.598
But every now and then, yeah,

00:11:59.598 --> 00:12:01.330
it's not going to hurt anything.

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:02.355
[Stan clears throat]

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:07.503
If you're craving a deli
sandwich or anything like that,

00:12:07.503 --> 00:12:09.010
it's important that you make sure

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:11.250
that the meat is hot.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:15.080
The big thing is avoiding any
sort of raw meat or raw fish.

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:16.525
Obviously she's overindulging,

00:12:16.525 --> 00:12:18.858
but I think it's cute. It's funny.

00:12:18.858 --> 00:12:20.641
So I'm gonna say 8 out of 10.

00:12:21.518 --> 00:12:23.784
[heartbeat pumping]

00:12:23.784 --> 00:12:25.645
That's the heartbeat.

00:12:25.645 --> 00:12:28.660
140 beats a minute, perfectly normal.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:31.130
When we're looking at fetal heart rate,

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.290
what's normal is anything between

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:36.800
110 beats per minute and
160 beats per minute.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:40.789
So a fetus' heartbeat is
definitely much faster,

00:12:40.789 --> 00:12:42.870
and that is completely normal and healthy.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.148
Even in the first trimester,

00:12:44.148 --> 00:12:45.598
that heartbeat can start pushing

00:12:45.598 --> 00:12:47.900
180 and 190 beats per minute,

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:49.350
and that's OK.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.640
The sonogram ultrasound
does look realistic.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:55.830
Probably not his hand
movements on the actual belly,

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.973
but the actual picture does look good.

00:12:59.859 --> 00:13:03.890
That little string of pearls.
That's the spine, yeah.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:05.720
He said, "Oh, look, the string of pearls."

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.260
That is not something we typically use

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:09.730
to describe the spine.

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:11.921
Oddly enough, we do kind of describe it

00:13:11.921 --> 00:13:13.146
as a railroad track,

00:13:13.146 --> 00:13:15.680
because you can kind of see two, you know,

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:17.750
it looks just like a railroad track.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:18.900
You gotta move back in with me.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:20.510
I want you off your feet
as much as possible.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:21.900
And no going to the lab.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:25.350
This is actually a
recommendation that is outdated.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:28.333
But before, doing bed rest

00:13:28.333 --> 00:13:30.991
and having someone stay off of their feet

00:13:30.991 --> 00:13:34.080
was kind of recommended all the time.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:35.988
Then a lot of these patients

00:13:35.988 --> 00:13:37.755
started developing blood clots

00:13:37.755 --> 00:13:39.620
in their legs or in their lungs.

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:41.460
And so they did a lot of research on it,

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:45.683
and actually having someone
who's pregnant on bed rest

00:13:45.683 --> 00:13:48.863
is, you know, increased
risk of blood clots.

00:13:48.863 --> 00:13:51.080
The whole premise and plot of this movie,

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:53.368
of having Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:13:53.368 --> 00:13:54.926
trying out this fertility drug

00:13:54.926 --> 00:13:58.726
and then consequently ending up pregnant,

00:13:58.726 --> 00:14:01.280
would be extremely unrealistic.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:05.020
The ultrasound, I thought,
was very realistic,

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:08.320
but as something that, is
it going to be possible now?

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:09.730
Probably not.

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:14.390
So I would say probably
a 3 or 4 out of 10.

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:16.115
That's not enough.

00:14:16.115 --> 00:14:17.070
What are you doing?!

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:19.500
I'm getting that baby out of you!

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:23.640
There's no real good data as to

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.523
any of these things that,

00:14:25.523 --> 00:14:26.838
maybe they're old wives' tales

00:14:26.838 --> 00:14:30.800
or things that you hear that
are gonna provoke labor.

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:33.511
Is it gonna hurt anything to try?

00:14:33.511 --> 00:14:35.030
No, not really.

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:36.046
If you feel comfortable

00:14:36.046 --> 00:14:38.546
and you find a comfortable
position for you

00:14:38.546 --> 00:14:41.810
and you want to try and
have sex to see, why not?

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:43.301
The cervix in pregnancy,

00:14:43.301 --> 00:14:45.151
which is the bottom part of the uterus,

00:14:45.151 --> 00:14:47.630
tends to be pretty sensitive in pregnancy.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:50.140
So anything that's going
to touch the cervix

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:51.240
or anything like that,

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:54.657
that can start cramping and contractions.

00:14:54.657 --> 00:14:56.515
Oh, no, no.

00:14:57.873 --> 00:14:59.206
I think my water just broke.

00:14:59.206 --> 00:15:00.710
The majority of women,

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:03.650
their water doesn't break spontaneously

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:05.270
like we see in the movies.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:06.640
This one's not quite as dramatic.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:08.620
Most women, you know, contractions start,

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.020
and their water just doesn't break.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:12.488
It'll break once they're in the hospital.

00:15:12.488 --> 00:15:14.713
Ross: OK, I got the pillow.

00:15:14.713 --> 00:15:16.030
Got the bag. You got the keys?

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:16.863
Rachel: I got the keys.

00:15:16.863 --> 00:15:20.192
I don't think there's any
sense for you to panic

00:15:20.192 --> 00:15:22.520
and, you know, rush and things like that.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:25.741
But you certainly have time
to carefully grab your bags

00:15:25.741 --> 00:15:28.480
and, you know, be rational
with your decisions,

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:30.130
get to the hospital safely.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:31.755
I think they do a really good job,

00:15:31.755 --> 00:15:34.047
and let's say ...

00:15:34.047 --> 00:15:36.577
7, 6 out of 10?

00:15:36.577 --> 00:15:38.159
This is your folic acid,

00:15:38.159 --> 00:15:40.142
and this is your prenatal vitamin.

00:15:40.142 --> 00:15:41.170
You're serious about this?

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:45.100
Definitely, this is the
most ridiculous size

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:47.800
prenatal vitamin I have ever seen.

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:49.440
If you have them that are this big,

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:50.960
please buy yourself another brand,

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:52.890
because this is just too much.

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:54.100
They're typically just, you know,

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:58.163
a regular vitamin, pill size.

00:15:58.163 --> 00:16:00.440
They even come in a gummy formation now,

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.498
which has all the same nutrients.

00:16:02.498 --> 00:16:05.481
It's actually encouraged and recommended

00:16:05.481 --> 00:16:07.930
to start taking a prenatal vitamin,

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:11.550
I mean, really for all women
who are of reproductive age.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:14.262
Even if you're not planning
on becoming pregnant,

00:16:14.262 --> 00:16:17.421
it actually helps the
development of a fetus.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:20.710
I can't do it.

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:23.001
I got it. It's going -- no.

00:16:23.001 --> 00:16:24.290
It is really funny,

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:28.520
but seeing a prenatal
vitamin that's that big,

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:31.860
not a thing. Or it shouldn't be a thing.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:33.333
So I would say 4 out of 10.

